  "result": "Neither, to start with. More agents **don't** always give better results, so begin with one.\n\nSay a student asks, \"Is the laser cutter free Friday? If so, book it.\" Booking depends on what the calendar check finds. If one agent checks and another books, the machine, date and time must be handed over correctly. That adds a place for mistakes without adding any new ability.\n\nA **multi-agent system** splits a job among several agents that pass work between them. It's tempting to picture more teammates getting more done. That can work when a job breaks into large, independent parts that can run side by side, such as broad research. It fits poorly when agents need the same information or depend on each other's steps ([Anthropic's account of its research system](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system)).\n\nIt also costs more. A **token** is a chunk of text, about a word or part of one, and model use is usually billed by the token. In Anthropic's data, agents used about 4 times as many tokens as ordinary chats, and multi-agent systems about 15 times. Your numbers will differ, but this matters on a small budget.\n\nThe general rule is to [use the simplest design that works](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents). For your design review: \"We start with one agent, test it on real student requests, and split it only if a specific failure shows that splitting would help.\"\n\nYour turn: which job is the better candidate for splitting, and why?\n\n- **A:** check the calendar, then book a free slot\n- **B:** search eight separate machine manuals for every eye protection rule\n\nI can also turn this into a short lesson on when multiple agents pay off.",
